{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "387",
    "case_summary": "A man in his late 60s presented for evaluation of a right iris lesion that had been noted during routine examination. Visual acuity was 20/25 OU. The eye was pseudophakic.\n\nSlitlamp examination showed a 10 × 9 × 3.5-mm, well-delineated, bilobed, vascularized, tan, peripheral iris nodule between the 3- and 6-o'clock positions. There were episcleral sentinel vessels and anterior chamber angle involvement but no visible iris seeding or aqueous cells.\n\n- Ultrasonographic biomicroscopy: Showed ciliary body involvement  - Fine-needle aspiration biopsy results:  - Showed monomorphic population of small to medium-sized hyperchromatic, CD20+ lymphocytes  - Few CD3+, reactive T lymphocytes were present  - Systemic evaluation: Showed no other involvement  - Staging: T2N0M0Images:- Image A: Well-delineated, bilobed, vascularized, tan iris lesion- Image B: Anterior chamber angle involvement on gonioscopy- Image C: Ciliary body involvement on ultrasonographic biomicroscopy- Image D: Aspiration biopsy showing neoplastic medium-sized lymphocytes with hyperchromatic nuclei- Image E: CD20+ neoplastic B lymphocytes immunohistochemistry- Image F: CD3+ T lymphocytes immunohistochemistry",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Unilateral Iris Mass with Possible Neoplastic Aetiology",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
